## 18. Queue Architecture

Use Redis + BullMQ.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    SCHED[Scheduler] --> Q1[job-ingestion]
    Q1 --> W1[Ingestion Workers]

    W1 --> Q2[job-normalization]
    Q2 --> W2[Normalization Workers]

    W2 --> Q3[job-dedup]
    Q3 --> W3[Dedup Workers]

    W3 --> Q4[job-matching]
    Q4 --> W4[Matching Workers]

    W4 --> Q5[resume-generation]
    Q5 --> W5[Resume Workers]

    W5 --> Q6[application-preparation]
    Q6 --> W6[Application Workers]

    W6 --> Q7[browser-automation]
    Q7 --> W7[Playwright Workers]
```

Each queue should support retries, backoff, concurrency limits, dead-letter handling, and idempotency.

## 19. Suggested Backend Services

For an initial implementation, these can remain in one Node.js application with modular workers.

```text
src/
├── modules/
│   ├── jobs/
│   ├── companies/
│   ├── sources/
│   ├── matching/
│   ├── resumes/
│   ├── applications/
│   ├── candidate/
│   └── analytics/
│
├── connectors/
│   ├── adzuna/
│   ├── jooble/
│   ├── greenhouse/
│   ├── lever/
│   ├── ashby/
│   ├── workable/
│   └── generic-crawler/
│
├── workers/
│   ├── ingestion.worker.ts
│   ├── normalization.worker.ts
│   ├── matching.worker.ts
│   ├── resume.worker.ts
│   └── application.worker.ts
│
└── infrastructure/
    ├── database/
    ├── redis/
    ├── storage/
    └── observability/
```

Do not split this into many microservices initially. Use a modular monolith plus workers. Split services only when scaling or isolation actually requires it.

